post_install() {
  systemctl --global enable hearthdeck.target

  cat <<'EOF'
Hearthdeck installs a systemd user target, API daemon, and host bridge socket.

The target is enabled globally and starts for users on their next login. The
Hearthdeck launcher also starts it for the current user. To start it immediately
without launching Hearthdeck, run as that user:

  systemctl --user daemon-reload
  systemctl --user start hearthdeck.target

The launcher pairs with the loopback daemon automatically. Optional LAN/TLS
configuration belongs in ~/.config/hearthdeck/daemon.env; see
/usr/share/doc/hearthdeck/daemon.env.example.

To receive future packages through pacman, follow the repository setup in
/usr/share/doc/hearthdeck/README.md. The current GitHub Pages repository is
unsigned and should not be enabled on systems that require signed packages.

If Hearthdeck was previously installed from a source checkout, move its copied
unit files out of ~/.config/systemd/user/ first. Those files override the
package units in /usr/lib/systemd/user. Preserve customizations as drop-ins.
EOF
}

post_upgrade() {
  post_install
}

post_remove() {
  systemctl --global disable hearthdeck.target

  cat <<'EOF'
If this was the final Hearthdeck package removal, disable its user services:
  systemctl --user disable --now hearthdeck.target hearthdeck-bridge.socket
EOF
}
